Raw dev notes -- The Hub's Got My Back (and Speaks Hebrew!)

Okay, so the "Personal Information Hub" project. This thing is basically my digital brain extension, a place where I can dump thoughts, get reminders, track stuff, and generally try to offload some of the mental overhead of daily life. It's all about making my personal info more accessible and actionable, without relying on a dozen different apps. I want it to be *my* hub, tailored exactly to how *I* think.

This past sprint, or whatever you want to call my chaotic personal dev time, was a mix of foundational reliability work and some genuinely exciting new features.

First up, the **host watchdog defense**. Man, this was a headache. The whole point of the Hub is that it's always there, always listening. But sometimes, things just... crash. Or hang. Or get stuck in some weird state. So I built this watchdog system – a sentinel process that monitors the main Hub application. If it detects a timeout, or if the main process just vanishes, it's supposed to restart it. The "restart fixes" in the commit message are a testament to how many times I thought I had it working, only for it to get into a restart loop, or fail to restart with the right environment, or just give up entirely. Debugging processes that die silently is a special kind of hell. But now, it feels pretty solid. It's a huge relief knowing the Hub can pick itself up if it stumbles.

Then came the fun stuff: **natural-language Hebrew reminders and the ability to remind other numbers**. This was a big one for me. I wanted to be able to just *tell* the Hub, in plain Hebrew, "תזכיר לי מחר בבוקר להתקשר לאמא" (Remind me tomorrow morning to call Mom), and have it just *work*. Parsing natural language, especially in Hebrew with its unique grammar and date/time expressions, was a beast. I spent a lot of time wrestling with regex and custom logic, trying to account for different ways people express time and dates. It's not perfect, but it's surprisingly good, and it feels like magic when it understands me. And the "remind-other-numbers" feature? That's just pure utility. Now I can tell it to remind my partner about something, or send a quick note to a friend, all through the same interface. It really expands the Hub's usefulness beyond just my own head.

Finally, I added some **host helper scripts and some dormant backend modules**. The helper scripts are just quality-of-life stuff for managing the server – quick ways to check logs, restart services, deploy updates. Nothing glamorous, but essential for keeping things running smoothly without pulling my hair out. The "dormant backend modules" are interesting. I built out some foundational pieces for future features, things I know I'll need down the line, but decided not to fully activate them yet. It's a bit of future-proofing, but also a lesson in scope management – don't build it all at once if you don't need it all right now. Sometimes it's better to lay the groundwork and let it sit for a bit.

Overall, this period felt really productive. I tackled some gnarly system-level reliability issues, which is always satisfying, and then got to dive into some really cool, user-facing features like the Hebrew NLP. It's a reminder that building robust systems is just as important as building shiny features. And seeing the Hub understand my native language? That's a special kind of personal victory.

Next up: Integrate some more data sources.
